# Solderi project authoring

When creating or changing a Solderi project, walkthrough, BOM, wiring diagram, firmware, or compatibility requirement:

- Read and follow `docs/PROJECT_AUTHORING.md` before editing.
- Use the existing walkthrough, catalogue, wiring-layout, and firmware files. A growing-sketch project stays a growing sketch. A single-firmware project stays one program. Do not invent a new project shape.
- Include every physical part the build needs, with a catalogue id and a quantity, so the hardware list can be built as written. Count jumper wires the way the layout uses them.
- Write the tutorial for a learner. Keep the main path to the finished build. Do not add a standalone lesson on an elementary idea unless the build cannot continue without it. Keep a short practical warning on the step that needs it, and keep essential safety, wiring, setup, troubleshooting, and checkpoints visible. Put longer background in `constants/walkthroughs/supporting-notes.ts` until a shared explanation control exists. Do not invent that control for one project, and do not build a Learn section while authoring a project. Do not repeat a parts list or explanation the walkthrough already gave. Keep development notes in the authoring record. Show a control only when it does something.
- Check the wiring table, diagram pins, instructions, code, and compatibility rows against each other. The same part and the same connection must use the same names.
- Give a breadboard project a physical hole layout and keep the schematic separate. Draw recognisable parts, real pin order, off-board connectors, and direct connector wires. Validate the holes against the netlist. Count individual male-to-male and male-to-female wires. Do not invent a breadboard for a project that has none. A multi-step build names, on each wiring step, the parts and jumpers that step installs, in an order the learner can follow with the circuit unpowered. The physical view keeps earlier parts, highlights the new ones, and hides the rest. If steps are removed or merged, renumber from 0 and move the assembly metadata with the instruction that still places those parts. Do not add a Current step or Complete build switch. Give every physical part a catalogue `identity`, a role, a stable id, and a recognisable shared illustration. Group repeated copies of one catalogue row under one parent with a quantity. Use `keyLabel` for the instance name, and do not group different electrical values by a generic name. A single part has no dropdown. Selecting an uninstalled instance does not reveal it. Selecting a part shows that detail inside the diagram card and does not leave the tutorial. Keep Components and Connections collapsed. Do not add another permanent diagram button. Connection rows come from the netlist and include only wires already introduced. A layout test, a drawing, and a bench build are three different claims.
- Keep enlarged-diagram zoom and pan inside a clipped viewport. Opening the viewer fits the diagram, and closing and reopening fits it again. Pinch stays under the fingers, including pinch-out, and one-finger drag pans only while zoomed. Keep the drawing from leaving the screen. Put Close inside the safe area. Do not add a Reset button. Switching Physical and Schematic fits that view. Fit different aspect ratios, return to the same walkthrough step, and do not call the viewer finished until it has been tried on a phone.
- Check that a published project is actually listed and can be opened. Drafts stay unpublished and must not cover the published list.
- Run `npm run test:compatibility`. `localAuthoredProjects` in `lib/authored-projects.ts` runs `reviewAuthoredProject` on every file in `constants/walkthroughs/`.
- When the project has one firmware sketch and a matching toolchain is installed, compile that sketch and record the result. If the toolchain is missing, say compilation was not run.
- Leave a new project unpublished. Publishing is a separate review.
- Say when behaviour was not run on a bench, and name any missing cover or other asset.
- When creating a project or changing its thumbnail, read and follow `docs/PROJECT_THUMBNAILS.md`. Match the existing workbench photographs. Show that project’s hardware. Register the JPEG on its own `PROJECT_IMAGES` key. A generated cover is not a bench test and is not the wiring diagram.
- A passing structural check does not prove the project works physically.
- Do not approve a component substitution. A substitution needs its own compatibility review id.

```ts
// Bad: mark a substitute as approved while writing the project
{ policy: 'direct', approvedReviewIds: [''] }

// Good: keep the requirement exact until a separate review exists
{ policy: 'exact', approvedReviewIds: [] }
```
